{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "query": "US China Phase One trade deal October 2019 status Trump Xi agricultural purchases",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-10-30",
      "to": "2019-10-30"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.axios.com/2019/10/17/us-china-trade-deal-trump-promises-tariffs-agriculture",
            "title": "Trump's promises on \"phase 1\" deal with China fall flat",
            "snippets": [
              "China isn't endorsing Trump's promises on the \"phase 1\" trade agreement.",
              "# Trump's promises on \"phase 1\" deal with China fall flat\nIt's been a week since President Trump touted his \"phase 1\" partial trade agreement with China as the greatest-ever deal for U.S. farmers — but China isn't endorsing his promises",
              "**Where it stands:** China has not confirmed Trump's claim that it will buy $40 billion–$50 billion worth of U.S. agricultural goods, and it says a final deal would require the U.S. to cancel all existing and future tariffs, CNBC reports. No final decision has been reached to determine if the U.S. will push tariff increases scheduled for Dec.",
              "- **Gao Feng, China’s Ministry of Commerce spokesperson**, did not say when the partial trade agreement between China and the U.S. would be signed or when future meetings on the unwritten plan will be held, per CNBC.\n- **Gao also said that China would increase** U.S. agricultural purchases based on market needs, but he did not specify figures",
              "- **A U.S. trade representative** spokesperson told the Washington Post that China had committed in the partial deal to buy $40 billion–$50 billion a year, “scaling up over two years.\"",
              "**The impact:** Farmer subsidies from the Trump administration aimed at mitigating the effects of the U.S.-China trade war reached $28 billion in September. U.S. farmers' personal income declined by an annualized $11.8 billion between January and March this year — the biggest Q1 drop in 3 years.\n\n**Go deeper:** Trump says U.S. and China reach partial trade agreement"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2019/10/25/us-and-china-close-to-finalising-parts-of-phase-one-trade-pact",
            "title": "US and China ‘close to finalising’ parts of phase-one trade ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# US and China ‘close to finalising’ parts of phase-one trade pact\n*Washington and Beijing have made progress in high-level discussions, although many points of contention remain.*\n\nU.S. Treasury Secretary Steve Mnuchin greets China''s Vice Premier Liu He\n\nMuch work still needs to be done on a comprehensive trade accord to resolve the long-running dispute between China and the United States [Yuri Gripas/Reuters]\nShare\nSave",
              "Trade officials from the United States and China are “close to finalising” some parts of an agreement after high-level telephone discussions on Friday, the US Trade Representative’s office said, adding that deputy-level talks would proceed “continuously”.\n\nBut in a statement issued after the call, the USTR provided no details on the areas of progress",
              "They made headway on specific issues and the two sides are close to finalising some sections of the agreement. Discussions will go on continuously at the deputy level, and the principals will have another call in the near future,” the statement said",
              "The call came as Washington and Beijing were working to agree on the text for a “phase one” trade agreement announced by US President Donald Trump on October 11. Trump has said he hopes to sign the deal with China’s President Xi Jinping next month in Chile at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit.\n\nBeijing was expected to request cancellation of some planned and existing US tariffs on Chinese imports during the phone call, people briefed on the negotiations told the Reuters news agency",
              "In return, China was expected to pledge to step up its purchases of US agricultural products.\n\nThe world’s two largest economies are trying to calm a nearly 16-month trade war that is roiling financial markets, disrupting supply chains and slowing global economic growth.\n\n“They want to make a deal very badly,” Trump told reporters at the White House. “They’re going to be buying much more farm products than anybody thought possible.”",
              "## Tariffs taken off the table?\nSo far, Trump has agreed only to cancel an October 15 increase in tariffs on $250bn in Chinese goods as part of understandings reached on agricultural purchases, increased access to China’s financial services markets, improved protections for intellectual property rights and a currency pact",
              "But to seal the deal, China is expected to ask the US to drop its plan to impose tariffs on $156bn worth of Chinese goods, including mobile phones, laptop computers and toys, on December 15",
              "Beijing also is likely to seek the removal of 15 percent tariffs imposed on September 1 on about $125bn of Chinese goods. Trump imposed the tariffs in August after a failed round of talks, effectively setting up punitive duties on nearly all of the $550bn in US imports from China",
              "The Chinese reportedly want to get back to tariffs on just the original $250bn in goods.\n\nDerek Scissors, a resident scholar and China expert at the American Enterprise Institute in Washington, said the original goal of the early October talks was to finalise a text on intellectual property, agriculture and market access to pave the way for a postponement of the December 15 tariffs",
              "“It’s odd that [Trump] was so upbeat with [Chinese Vice Premier] Liu He and yet we still don’t have the December 15 tariffs taken off the table,” Scissors said.\n\nUS Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin last week said no decisions were made about the December 15 tariffs, but added: “We’ll address that as we continue to have conversations”",
              "## ‘Constructive’ relationship\nIf a text can be sealed, Beijing in return would exempt some US agricultural products from tariffs, including soya beans, wheat and corn, a China-based source told Reuters.\n\nBuyers would be exempt from extra tariffs for future buying and get returns for tariffs they already paid in previous purchases of the products on the list. But the ultimate amounts of China’s purchases are uncertain",
              "Trump has touted purchases of up to $50bn annually – far above China’s 2017 purchases of $19.5bn, as measured by the American Farm Bureau Federation.\n\nOne of the sources briefed on the talks said China’s offer would start at around $20bn in annual purchases, largely restoring the pre-trade-war status quo – yet this could rise over time. Purchases also would depend on market conditions and pricing",
              "US Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer has emphasised China’s agreement to remove some restrictions on US genetically modified crops and other food safety barriers, which the sources said is significant because it could pave the way for much higher US farm exports to China.\n\nAdvertisement",
              "The high-level call came a day after US Vice President Mike Pence railed against China’s trade practices and what he termed construction of a “surveillance state” in a major policy speech. But Pence left the door open to a trade deal with China, saying Trump wanted a “constructive” relationship with Beijing",
              "While the US tariffs on Chinese goods have brought China to the negotiating table to address US grievances over its trade practices and intellectual property practices, they have so far failed to lead to significant changes in China’s state-led economic model.\n\nThe “phase one” deal will ease tensions and provide some market stability, but is expected to do little to deal with core US complaints about Chinese theft and forced transfer of US intellectual property and technology",
              "The intellectual property rights chapter in the agreement largely deals with copyright and trademark issues – and pledges to curb technology transfers that Beijing has already put into a new investment law, people familiar with the discussions said.\n\nMore difficult issues, including data restrictions, China’s cybersecurity regulations and industrial subsidies will be left for later phases of talks.\n\nBut some China trade experts said that a completion of a phase-one deal could leave little incentive for China to negotiate further, especially with a US presidential election in 2020",
              "{\"headline\":\"US and China ‘close to finalising’ parts of phase-one trade pact\",\"author\":{\"foundingDate\":\"2006-11-15\",\"name\":\"Al Jazeera\",\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/aljazeera\",\"https://twitter.com/ajenglish\",\"https://www.youtube.com/aljazeeraenglish\",\"https://www.instagram.com/aljazeeraenglish/?hl=en\"]},\"publisher\":{\"foundingDate\":\"2006-11-15\",\"name\":\"Al Jazeera\",\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/aljazeera\",\"https://twitter.com/ajenglish\",\"https://www.youtube.com/aljazeeraenglish\",\"https://www.instagram.com/aljazeeraenglish/?hl=en\"]},\"datePublished\":\"2019-10-25T18:39:30Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-10-25T19:00:01Z\"}"
            ]
